    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This work describes a study of CO2/hydrocarbon phase behavior using supercritical fluid chromatography (SFC). Four applications are examined. First, SFC is used to determine CO2/hydrocarbon binary interaction parameters for the Peng-Robinson equation of state (EOS). An equation is derived that relates the SFC elution times of a hydrocarbon to its binary interaction parameter with CO2. Binary interaction parameters for several n-alkanes are measured using SFC. The next application is the characterization of crude oils for predictions of phase behavior of CO2/crude oil mixtures with an EOS. SFC with a CO2 carrier is used in two ways. SFC is used to measure the composition of an oil using simulated distillation. In addition, unknown CO2/hydrocarbon binary interaction parameters, which are needed for the EOS calculations, are determined using the method mentioned before. The proposed characterization procedure is tested for four CO2/crude oil systems. Reported are comparisons of predicted phase behavior with pressure-volume temperature observations for mixtures of CO2 and oil from the Means, Maljamar, Wasson, and Kubiki fields. The feasibility of using carrier fluids other than CO2 is investigated by performing some chromatography experiments with a N2 carrier fluid. Finally, SFC is used to determine the solubility of a hydrocarbon in dense CO2.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
